# AutoDock Vina — Apptainer definition.
#
# Molecular docking: scores and optimises the pose of a small-molecule ligand against a
# rigid (or partly flexible) receptor. Packaged here with Meeko, which is what turns an
# ordinary structure file into the PDBQT format Vina actually reads.
#
#   apptainer build --fakeroot vina.sif vina.def
#   apptainer build --fakeroot --build-arg VINA_PYTHON=no vina-cli.sif vina.def
#
# THIS CONTAINER DOES NOT USE A GPU. Vina is a CPU docking engine and scales with CPU
# cores, not cards. Run it without `--nv`, and ask the scheduler for cores rather than a
# GPU; `--exhaustiveness` and `--cpu` are the knobs that matter. (AutoDock-GPU is a
# different program and is not packaged here.)
#
# Unlike every other image in this repository, this one is ARCHITECTURE-PORTABLE. Upstream
# publishes static linux binaries for both x86_64 and aarch64, and the %post below picks by
# `uname -m`, so the same definition builds a working image on an ARM machine such as a
# GH200 node. Build it on the architecture you will run it on; nothing here cross-builds.
#
# About the Python bindings (VINA_PYTHON, default yes): scripting a docking sweep through
# the Python API is a common reason to want Vina in a container at all. On x86_64 this is
# just an install — vina 1.2.7 publishes manylinux and musllinux wheels for cp38 to cp312,
# with Boost vendored inside them, so there is no toolchain and no runtime library to
# manage. On ARM there is no wheel, so the extension is compiled from source and Boost and
# SWIG are installed and kept. If a future rebuild breaks on that, `--build-arg
# VINA_PYTHON=no` gives a smaller image with the command-line tools intact.

BootStrap: docker
From: python:3.12.12-slim-bookworm

%arguments
    VINA_PYTHON=yes

%labels
    Maintainer  Benedikt Singer
    Tool        AutoDock Vina
    Upstream    https://github.com/ccsb-scripps/AutoDock-Vina
    Version     1.2.7
    Meeko       0.8.0
    License     Apache-2.0

%environment
    export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1
    export PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1
    export XDG_CACHE_HOME=/tmp/vina-cache
    export MPLCONFIGDIR=/tmp/vina-cache/mpl

%post
    set -eux
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    export PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1

    VINA_VERSION=1.2.7
    MEEKO_VERSION=0.8.0
    VINA_PYTHON={{ VINA_PYTHON }}

    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends ca-certificates wget
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    # Upstream's release assets are named by platform. Select by the build machine's
    # architecture rather than assuming x86_64, so this definition also builds on ARM.
    case "$(uname -m)" in
        x86_64)          VINA_ARCH=linux_x86_64 ;;
        aarch64|arm64)   VINA_ARCH=linux_aarch64 ;;
        *) echo "no upstream Vina binary for $(uname -m)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac

    BASE="https://github.com/ccsb-scripps/AutoDock-Vina/releases/download/v${VINA_VERSION}"
    wget -q -O /usr/local/bin/vina       "${BASE}/vina_${VINA_VERSION}_${VINA_ARCH}"
    wget -q -O /usr/local/bin/vina_split "${BASE}/vina_split_${VINA_VERSION}_${VINA_ARCH}"
    chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/vina /usr/local/bin/vina_split

    # These are prebuilt binaries off a release page, so prove they actually execute on this
    # architecture now, at build time. A wrong-architecture or truncated download otherwise
    # only surfaces as "Exec format error" inside somebody's job.
    /usr/local/bin/vina --version
    /usr/local/bin/vina_split --help > /dev/null 2>&1 || true

    python -m pip install --no-cache-dir "uv>=0.13.4"

    # Meeko prepares both ligands and receptors as PDBQT and writes docked poses back out to
    # SDF. RDKit is its chemistry backend and is what reads SMILES, SDF and MOL2.
    #
    # EVERY ONE OF THESE IS LISTED BY HAND BECAUSE MEEKO DECLARES NO DEPENDENCIES AT ALL:
    # its wheel metadata has an empty requires_dist, so `pip install meeko` pulls in
    # nothing and then `import meeko` dies with ModuleNotFoundError for whichever one is
    # missing (gemmi, reached through meeko.polymer -> meeko.chemtempgen, is the first).
    # If a future meeko adds an import, this list is where it goes, and %test is what
    # catches the omission.
    #
    # prody is needed by mk_prepare_receptor.py specifically -- the ligand path works
    # without it, so a build that omits it looks fine right up to the first receptor.
    #
    # Deliberately NOT installed: torch, openff/espaloma, AutoDockTools. Meeko
    # imports those only on optional paths (force-field parameterisation, reactive
    # docking) and each is large enough to change the size of this image materially.
    uv pip install --system --no-cache \
        "meeko==${MEEKO_VERSION}" \
        "rdkit" \
        "numpy" \
        "scipy" \
        "gemmi" \
        "pandas" \
        "prody"

    case "${VINA_PYTHON}" in
        yes)
            case "$(uname -m)" in
                x86_64)
                    # A wheel exists for this interpreter (cp38-cp312, manylinux_2_17 and
                    # musllinux_1_2), with Boost vendored in by auditwheel. Nothing is
                    # compiled and nothing has to stay behind in the image.
                    uv pip install --system --no-cache "vina==${VINA_VERSION}"
                    ;;
                aarch64|arm64)
                    # No ARM wheel is published, so the extension is compiled here and
                    # Boost and SWIG are installed for it. They are deliberately NOT
                    # purged afterwards: the extension links against the Boost runtime
                    # libraries, and `apt-get autoremove` takes those with the -dev
                    # packages, leaving an import that fails with
                    # "libboost_filesystem.so.1.74.0: cannot open shared object file".
                    apt-get update
                    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
                        build-essential swig libboost-all-dev
                    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
                    # LD_PRELOAD is dropped for the compile on purpose. Apptainer injects
                    # its libfakeroot interposer into every process in %post, and during a
                    # C++ build of this size it intermittently corrupts object files: the
                    # link then dies with "file format not recognized" on a .o, after a
                    # wall of "libfakeroot internal error: payload not recognized!".
                    # Compiling needs no faked root, so taking the interposer out of the
                    # way for this one command is both safe and sufficient.
                    env -u LD_PRELOAD uv pip install --system --no-cache \
                        "vina==${VINA_VERSION}"
                    ;;
                *)
                    echo "no Vina python bindings for $(uname -m)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
            esac
            python -c "import vina; print('vina python bindings OK', vina.__version__)"
            ;;
        no)
            echo "skipping python bindings (VINA_PYTHON=no); command-line tools only"
            ;;
        *)
            echo "VINA_PYTHON must be yes or no (got '${VINA_PYTHON}')" >&2
            exit 2
            ;;
    esac

%test
    set -eu
    vina --version
    python -c "import meeko, rdkit; print('meeko', meeko.__version__, 'rdkit', rdkit.__version__)"
    command -v mk_prepare_ligand.py > /dev/null && echo "meeko CLI present"

    # A real docking run on a ligand and receptor built here, so the test covers the whole
    # chain: RDKit reads SMILES, Meeko writes PDBQT, Vina scores poses. A passing
    # `vina --version` says nothing about whether docking works.
    T="$(mktemp -d)"
    python - "$T" <<'RDKEOF'
import sys

from rdkit import Chem
from rdkit.Chem import AllChem

root = sys.argv[1]

# Aspirin: small, with a couple of rotatable bonds, so the search has something to do.
mol = Chem.AddHs(Chem.MolFromSmiles("CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1C(=O)O"))
AllChem.EmbedMolecule(mol, randomSeed=0xF00D)
AllChem.MMFFOptimizeMolecule(mol)
Chem.MolToMolFile(mol, root + "/ligand.sdf")

# A one-residue receptor, written by hand. This is a plumbing test, not a meaningful
# docking: what is being checked is that each tool reads what the previous one wrote.
pdb = """ATOM      1  N   ALA A   1      10.000  10.000  10.000  1.00  0.00           N
ATOM      2  CA  ALA A   1      11.458  10.000  10.000  1.00  0.00           C
ATOM      3  C   ALA A   1      12.009  11.420  10.000  1.00  0.00           C
ATOM      4  O   ALA A   1      11.273  12.390  10.000  1.00  0.00           O
ATOM      5  CB  ALA A   1      11.979   9.235  11.213  1.00  0.00           C
TER
END
"""
with open(root + "/receptor.pdb", "w") as handle:
    handle.write(pdb)
print("built ligand.sdf and receptor.pdb")
RDKEOF

    mk_prepare_ligand.py -i "$T/ligand.sdf" -o "$T/ligand.pdbqt"
    test -s "$T/ligand.pdbqt"

    # The receptor box IS the search space. Getting it wrong is the commonest way a
    # docking run returns a confident number about the wrong part of the protein.
    mk_prepare_receptor.py -i "$T/receptor.pdb" -o "$T/receptor" -p \
        --box_center 11.5 10.5 10.5 --box_size 16 16 16 > /dev/null

    vina --receptor "$T/receptor.pdbqt" --ligand "$T/ligand.pdbqt" \
        --center_x 11.5 --center_y 10.5 --center_z 10.5 \
        --size_x 16 --size_y 16 --size_z 16 \
        --exhaustiveness 2 --seed 42 --cpu 2 \
        --out "$T/docked.pdbqt" | tail -12
    grep -q "REMARK VINA RESULT" "$T/docked.pdbqt"
    rm -rf "$T"
    echo "VINA_TEST_OK"

%runscript
    exec vina "$@"

%help
    AutoDock Vina 1.2.7 with Meeko. CPU only — do not pass --nv.

    Vina reads PDBQT, which is not a format anything else writes, so preparation is a real
    step rather than a formality:

      # ligand: SDF/MOL2/SMILES -> PDBQT
      apptainer exec --bind "$PWD":/data vina.sif \
          mk_prepare_ligand.py -i /data/ligand.sdf -o /data/ligand.pdbqt

      # receptor: PDB/mmCIF -> PDBQT (strip waters and other unwanted heteroatoms first)
      apptainer exec --bind "$PWD":/data vina.sif \
          mk_prepare_receptor.py -i /data/receptor.pdb -o /data/receptor \
          --box_center 11.0 22.0 33.0 --box_size 20 20 20

      # dock
      apptainer run --bind "$PWD":/data vina.sif \
          --receptor /data/receptor.pdbqt \
          --ligand /data/ligand.pdbqt \
          --config /data/receptor.box.txt \
          --exhaustiveness 32 \
          --cpu 8 \
          --out /data/docked.pdbqt

      # split a multi-model result into one file per pose
      apptainer exec --bind "$PWD":/data vina.sif vina_split --input /data/docked.pdbqt

    THE SEARCH BOX IS THE RESULT. Vina only searches inside the box you give it, so a box
    that misses the pocket returns confident nonsense rather than an error. Derive the
    centre from a known ligand or a pocket prediction, and check it before a large sweep.

    ON A CLUSTER: ask for cores, not a GPU. `--cpu N` should match the cores the scheduler
    actually gave you; left unset Vina reads the machine's core count, which on a shared
    node is far more than your allocation and oversubscribes it.

    Reproducibility: pass `--seed` for a repeatable run. Without it, Vina seeds from the
    clock and successive runs differ.

    Apache-2.0 (Vina). Meeko is LGPL-2.1 and RDKit is BSD-3-Clause.
